**Overview of the situation**  
The speaker appears to be a middle-aged man (likely in his 40s or 50s, given he says he started working at 13) who has worked his way up from manual labour. He is frustrated with young job seekers who complain about not finding work, yet allegedly demand cash-in-hand wages, lack experience and qualifications, and reject jobs as “hard, dirty, low-paid, or with no prospects.” He concludes that the real reason companies can’t hire people is not that the offers are bad, but that the young people themselves are the problem.

**Objective problems he has run into**  
He is facing a generational and cultural gap: he sees a mismatch between what young workers expect and what employers (especially small shops or labour-intensive industries) are willing to offer. He is also likely running a small business or working in a trade where he has tried to hire and failed, leading to his frustration. His deeper problem is that he cannot understand why young people don’t share his own survival-driven work ethic, and he interprets their pickiness as a moral failing rather than a rational response to changed economic conditions.

**Whether I agree with his conclusion**  
I partially disagree. His observation that some young people lack experience or qualifications and still demand premium conditions is true in certain cases, but it is an overgeneralisation. Many young workers today face a genuinely different labour market: wages have stagnated relative to housing costs, many “entry-level” jobs are precarious (zero-hour contracts, no training, no progression), and cash-in-hand work often means no legal protection, no pension, and no sick pay — which is objectively worse for the worker, not a privilege. His own experience at 13 was in a different era with different costs of living and social expectations. So while his frustration is understandable, his conclusion that “the people are the problem” ignores structural factors such as low real wages, poor working conditions, and the erosion of job security. A fairer conclusion would be that both sides need to adjust: employers should offer more transparent, secure terms, and some young people should be more realistic about entry-level expectations — but blaming an entire generation is neither accurate nor helpful. [end of text]


